Archimedes keyboard: carry the mouse delta instead of truncating it.

WHY

src/devices/machine/archimedes_keyb.cpp emits the mouse as real quadrature.
Its `update_mouse` timer runs at 4 kHz and, on any non-zero delta, advances
the X (and Y) quadrature phase by exactly ONE step -- then assigns

    m_mouse_x = x;      // x = m_mouse[0]->read(), the ioport position

so every count beyond the first is DISCARDED. With a host mouse that is
invisible: SDL never delivers more than a count or two per 250 us tick, so
the sampled delta is almost always 1 and nothing is lost.

A PROGRAMMATIC driver is the opposite shape. kernel-hive's ctlsock engine
states a whole movement into the analog ioport and lets the device difference
it, exactly as the SGI, ADB and MSX pointers do. MEASURED on riscos3
2026-09-20, RISC OS 3.11 Desktop, MAME 0.289 aa310: a 600-count MOVE advanced
the device's own m_mouse_x from 0 to 900 and moved the guest pointer ZERO
pixels -- one quadrature step for the whole flight. Pacing the counts one per
emulated millisecond does deliver them (the pointer moves, 1.493 VIDC cursor
units per count), but it caps a MOVEA round at MAME_CTL_MOVE_STEP=1 count,
and the closed loop then gives up long before it reaches a target on the far
side of the screen: ten targets, ten `giveups`, worst error 332 px.

WHAT IT CHANGES

Two lines at the end of `update_mouse`: advance the recorded position by ONE
unit per tick toward the sampled one rather than jumping to it, and keep the
remainder for the next tick. The motion is delivered WHOLE, a few ticks later,
at the device's own 4 kHz rate -- 4000 counts/s, far beyond any hand, and far
beyond the ~1000/s an unpaced host mouse could ever produce. This is the same
correction, for the same reason, as the fleet's mame-hle-ps2-mouse-carry.patch
(SGI's 9-bit PS/2 report field).

The int16_t difference is deliberate: the ioport is a 16-bit WRAPPING
accumulator (PORT_BIT(0xffff, IPT_MOUSE_X)) and the engine's running total
wraps in that space, so the direction has to be read from the SIGNED 16-bit
difference, exactly as the upstream `int16_t dx = x - m_mouse_x` already does.

CONTAINMENT

  * Host-mouse behaviour is unchanged in practice: a per-tick delta of 1
    takes the same path it always did.
  * No save items are added, removed or renamed (m_mouse_x/m_mouse_y are
    already saved), so the ctlsock module's savestate signature and every
    golden baked against it are untouched.
  * Touches one file and nothing else.

MEASURED AFTER (riscos3, same rig): MAME_CTL_MOVE_STEP back at the fleet
default, MOVEA converges on all five spread targets -- see
docs/lab/RISCOS3-WAVE.md for the accuracy table.
--- a/src/devices/machine/archimedes_keyb.cpp
+++ b/src/devices/machine/archimedes_keyb.cpp
@@ -381,8 +381,22 @@
 		m_mouse_yphase &= 3;
 	}
 
-	m_mouse_x = x;
-	m_mouse_y = y;
+	// [kernel-hive] CARRY, DON'T TRUNCATE. Upstream assigns the sampled
+	// position here, so a delta of N counts arriving between two ticks emits
+	// exactly ONE quadrature step and throws N-1 away -- the guest's pointer
+	// then moves one unit no matter how far the mouse travelled. That is
+	// invisible with a real host mouse (which never delivers more than a
+	// count or two per 250 us tick) and fatal to a programmatic driver, which
+	// states a whole movement at once: kernel-hive's ctlsock engine issues a
+	// per-round budget and measured ZERO pointer motion for a 600-count move.
+	// Advance by ONE unit per tick instead and keep the remainder: the motion
+	// is delivered whole, a few ticks later, at the device's own 4 kHz rate
+	// (4000 counts/s, well beyond any hand). Same shape as the fleet's
+	// mame-hle-ps2-mouse-carry.patch. No save items change.
+	if (m_mouse_x != x)
+		m_mouse_x += (int16_t(x - m_mouse_x) > 0) ? 1 : -1;
+	if (m_mouse_y != y)
+		m_mouse_y += (int16_t(y - m_mouse_y) > 0) ? 1 : -1;
 }
 
 void archimedes_keyboard_device::leds_w(uint8_t data)
